  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Opioid use disorder on buprenorphine (F11.20)</content>. No opioid use, but cravings persist at 8 mg. Urine test today shows buprenorphine and its metabolite and no fentanyl or other opioids. Dr. Ortiz agrees with increasing the dose. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; naloxone in place. [S]<list><item>Increase buprenorphine with naloxone to 16 mg (two films) once daily.</item><item>Fiber, fluids, and a stool softener as needed for constipation.</item><item>Weekly counseling and a peer recovery coach, as she chose today.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder (F10.20)</content>. Drinking unchanged. Naltrexone cannot be used with buprenorphine; kidney function is normal (creatinine 0.72 mg/dL), so acamprosate is suitable. [S]<list><item>Start acamprosate 666 mg three times daily.</item><item>Continue thiamine.</item><item>PEth and liver tests at the next visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Screening results</content>. Hepatitis C antibody nonreactive (signal-to-cutoff 0.09); hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody nonreactive, and surface antibody 3 mIU/mL, so she is not immune; HIV antigen-antibody test nonreactive. AST 52 and GGT 84 U/L with ALT 38, consistent with alcohol; albumin 4.0 g/dL and bilirubin 0.7 mg/dL normal; FIB-4 1.18, below 1.3, which makes advanced fibrosis unlikely. [S]<list><item>Second hepatitis B vaccine dose at the next visit.</item><item>Repeat liver tests with PEth.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Buprenorphine dose, adherence, cravings, withdrawal, and side effects</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>8 mg daily, every dose taken; evening cravings; no withdrawal or sedation; increased to 16 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Visit frequency</td><td>Every 1 to 2 weeks in the first month after starting, then every 2 to 4 weeks until stable</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen one week after starting</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine drug testing, including fentanyl, explained as part of care rather than as a test of trust</td><td>At the start, then about monthly early in treatment and as needed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Buprenorphine and norbuprenorphine present; no fentanyl or other opioids</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Prescription drug monitoring program check</td><td>Before each buprenorphine prescription</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No other controlled-substance prescriptions</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone at home and in her bag, and a trained companion</td><td>At the start; check and replace at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two two-packs dispensed; her friend trained</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Overdose prevention: never using alone and fentanyl test strips</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed; fentanyl test strips given</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Counseling and peer recovery support</td><td>Offered to everyone; not a condition of medication</td><td>Completed today</td><td>She chose weekly counseling and a peer recovery coach</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Oral care on sublingual buprenorphine</td><td>At the start and yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>After the film dissolves, swish water and swallow, and wait an hour before brushing; dental visit advised</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Drinking and PEth</td><td>PEth at intake and at each check while drinking continues; AUDIT-C yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PEth 312 ng/mL and AUDIT-C 8 a week ago; four or five drinks on performance nights</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acamprosate adherence and kidney function</td><td>Kidney function before starting; adherence every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Creatinine 0.72 mg/dL and eGFR 113 a week ago; acamprosate started</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naltrexone for alcohol use disorder</td><td>First-line for alcohol use disorder unless opioids are needed</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Contraindicated with buprenorphine</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Alcohol withdrawal risk</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No history of withdrawal seizures or delirium; no withdrawal symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Thiamine</td><td>While drinking heavily</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taking thiamine 100 mg daily</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (AST, ALT, GGT, albumin, bilirubin)</td><td>At intake, then every 1 to 3 months while drinking is heavy, and yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>AST 52, ALT 38, GGT 84 U/L a week ago; albumin and bilirubin normal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>FIB-4 fibrosis score</td><td>At intake; repeat in 6 to 12 months with ongoing drinking, and every 1 to 2 years</td><td>Up to date</td><td>1.18 a week ago (age 33, AST 52, ALT 38, platelets 236): low risk</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Vibration-controlled transient elastography</td><td>If FIB-4 is 1.3 or higher</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>FIB-4 below 1.3</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis C, hepatitis B, and HIV testing</td><td>At entry to care, then yearly while any drug use continues</td><td>Up to date</td><td>All nonreactive a week ago</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Hepatitis A and B vaccines</td><td>Hepatitis B two doses 4 weeks apart; hepatitis A two doses 6 months apart</td><td>Up to date</td><td>First doses a week ago; hepatitis B dose 2 next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake and at each recovery visit while symptoms are present</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 9 a week ago; next at the five-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide and overdose risk review, safety plan, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; overdose risk lowered by buprenorphine and naloxone; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline saved in her phone</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Pregnancy test and contraception</td><td>Before starting buprenorphine; contraception discussed</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Urine pregnancy test negative a week ago</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Addiction medicine consultation</td><td>At the start of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Ortiz today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Stay on buprenorphine without interruption through six months, including through any return to use. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Naloxone at home and in her bag, and a friend trained to use it, by the one-week visit. Outcome by 2026-04-27: Two naloxone two-packs dispensed; her friend trained [S]</item><item>Reduce drinking to PEth below 20 ng/mL, or to the level she chooses, by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>Hepatitis A and B vaccine series completed by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 score below 5 by six months. In progress; target 2026-10-19 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The ASAM National Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Opioid Use Disorder: 2020 Focused Update (2020); Treatment Options for Substance Use Disorder (medications for opioid and alcohol use disorders) (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Substance Use Disorders (2021); Diagnosis and Treatment of Alcohol-Associated Liver Diseases: 2019 Practice Guidance From the American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases (2019); AASLD Practice Guideline on blood-based noninvasive liver disease assessment of hepatic fibrosis and steatosis (2025); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Visit in two weeks; she leaves on tour in five days and will call in, with the in-person visit when she returns. Counselor weekly. Results by phone. Next scheduled visit 2026-05-26: five-week recovery review and second hepatitis B vaccine. [S]</paragraph></text>

